    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Catie Fee’s human rights capstone project examined the attitudes and perceptions of public defenders in relation to their clients and the court system and argues that public defenders see it as their responsibility to humanize their clients in conjunction with and beyond their role as a legal advocate. Through existing literature and in-depth interviews with public defenders, Catie describes first-hand accounts of mistreatment in courts and awareness of structural dehumanization further demonstrating how these perceptions are filtered through public defender narratives. Furthermore, Catie analyzed the mechanisms through which public defenders humanize their clients such as presenting legally relevant and humanizing materials during post-trial sentencing mitigation, preserving positive courtroom atmospherics, providing holistic council, and using case strategy to center the client and their agency.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fee, C. (2024). Humanizing the Defendant: Public Defenders’ Perspectives on Their Responsibility in US Criminal Courts. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sb946tq8528. https://doi.org/10.25740/sb946tq8528.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
